# Virtual input devices ghost creates for its sessions (docs/design/audio-cursor-gamepad.md#gamepads; host/ghostseat/src/devices.rs).
#
# A ghost session has no seat, and by default a virtual controller gets
# the opposite of what it needs: udev's input_id tags it
# ID_INPUT_JOYSTICK, 70-uaccess.rules turns that into a `uaccess` tag
# (and Steam's 60-steam-input.rules tags Sony and Valve hidraw nodes
# too), a virtual device has no seated parent so 73-seat-late.rules
# leaves it on seat0, and logind then ACLs the nodes to whoever is
# sitting at the console -- who also sees a phantom controller appear --
# while the session user they were created for can't open them at all.
#
# These rules take ghost's devices off seat0 and out of uaccess, and
# drop `power-switch` so logind never acts on one. They decide nothing
# about who owns them: ghostseat chowns each node to its session's uid
# after udev has processed it (which is why MODE is set here -- udev
# applies it on the add event, and a chown that raced ahead of it would
# be reset). Keyed on the phys ghostseat sets, which only it can since only
# root can open /dev/uinput and /dev/uhid:
#
# - a uinput pad carries it on its input device (ATTRS{phys});
# - a uhid controller carries it on its HID device as HID_PHYS, which
#   its driver's input devices don't copy (their phys is empty), so the
#   second line matches the HID parent's uevent instead, for its input
#   and hidraw nodes alike.
#
# Numbered 72: after 70-uaccess (so TAG-= sticks), before 73-seat-late (so
# ID_SEAT is respected). ghostseat checks that this file is installed,
# and for raw controllers that it has the second line, and reports
# gamepad forwarding unavailable if not -- see its log at session start.
SUBSYSTEM=="input", ATTRS{phys}=="ghost/*", ENV{ID_SEAT}="ghost", TAG-="uaccess", TAG-="seat", TAG-="power-switch", MODE="0600"
SUBSYSTEM=="input|hidraw", ATTRS{uevent}=="*HID_PHYS=ghost/*", ENV{ID_SEAT}="ghost", TAG-="uaccess", TAG-="seat", TAG-="power-switch", MODE="0600"
